+
+The original table uses **in-harness policy-family baselines**, not independent upstream
+replications. This harness uses K-as-Q prefill scoring and one token ranking
+averaged across heads for H2O and SnapKV; upstream versions use real query
+or attention signals and per-head selection. The harness also adds sink
+pinning to SnapKV and uses H2O-style decode eviction for it. Baseline
+fidelity remains open in the review checklist; treat numeric comparisons as
+internal controls, not definitive paper-to-paper rankings.
+
+The inclusive-shadow policy avoided **2,510,023 of 2,849,120 potential
+decode-demotion writes (88.1%)** across these samples. This counter excludes
+initial prompt placement in the slow tier. It is a simulated event ratio,
+not a measured STT-RAM write rate or a paired no-shadow experiment: the
+"potential" count assumes the same promotion/eviction sequence without
+reusing an existing backup.
+Each count is one layer-token (4 KiB bf16 K/V), so the saved payload is
+**10.28 GB across 135 requests**, or 72.6 MiB/request. At the model's assumed
+12.5 GB/s write bandwidth, that is only **0.154 ms of ideal write-transfer
+time per generated token**; at its assumed 8 pJ/bit it is 0.658 J across the
+run, less than 1% of modeled decode dynamic energy. The same prompt lengths
+would initially place about 25.90 GB into the hypothetical slow tier, so the
+avoided fraction of *potential total slow-tier writes including prefill* is
+about 27.4%. These are derived counterfactuals, not hardware measurements.
+Likewise, the current analytical energy counters cover decode KV traffic;
+they omit prompt placement, model compute, and system power and are not
+whole-request energy measurements.
+
+A full paired no-shadow comparison on the same 135 input requests found
+**88.1% fewer paid decode-demotion writes per decode step** with inclusive
+backups (10.58 GB less aggregate bf16 layer-token payload). However,
+task-balanced quality was **−0.20 points** with shadows, paired conditional
+95% interval [−0.47, +0.07]; GovReport was 26.93 versus 28.36 ROUGE-L on
+10 samples. Both arms used the same source, samples, seed, and capacities;
+the inclusive arm reproduces the frozen v3 control exactly. A same-GPU,
+real-text 16k ABBA check measured 117.17 versus 135.82 ms/token full-loop
+prototype latency (inclusive versus no-shadow), with unchanged 18.59 GB
+prefill peak. See `results/shadow_ablation_full/report.json`. The write
+counters are simulated, and this speed difference is from the Python/GPU
+prototype—not measured STT hardware. The two arms generated different output
+lengths, so the per-step write rate is fairer than the aggregate count.
