+**One sentence to remember:** TieredKV keeps a limited set of old tokens
+available to the model and tests whether a backup copy can avoid writing the
+same token to slow memory again. The current main program simulates that slow
+memory on the GPU.
+
+## The idea in one minute
+
+A language model reads a prompt and then writes an answer one word-piece at a
+time. To avoid rereading the whole prompt for every new piece, it saves small
+pieces of information about earlier tokens. Those saved pieces are called the
+**key–value cache**, or **KV cache**.
+
+A long prompt creates a large KV cache. The fast memory attached to the graphics
+processor (**GPU memory**, also called **VRAM**) is limited. TieredKV tries to
+keep the most useful saved tokens in a small *fast* group and retain some other
+tokens in a larger *slow* group. If a slow token becomes useful, the policy can
+bring it back to the fast group. If both groups are full, some tokens are lost.
+
+**The crucial reality:** in the normal evaluated program, both groups are
+physically stored on the same GPU. “Slow STT-RAM” is currently a *model of a
+future device*, not an installed device. An opt-in backend stores cold KV in ordinary CPU memory and batches transfers
+and score updates. It improves speed over the same-placement GPU version,
+but the corrected shared-ceiling comparison remains slower than SnapKV
+(about 71 versus 26 ms/token). CPU DRAM is not physical STT-RAM.
+
+## Five basics before the algorithm
+
+1. **Token:** a small piece of text. A word may contain one or several tokens.
+2. **Attention:** the model's way of using relevant earlier tokens when
+   predicting the next token. Each model layer has its own saved keys and
+   values for earlier tokens.
+3. **Prefill:** the first pass over the prompt. The current default computes
+   the complete prompt KV on the GPU *before* deciding what to retain.
+4. **Decode:** the repeated steps that generate the answer. Each step uses
+   saved KV, produces a new token, and adds new KV.
+5. **Capacity:** the maximum number of saved tokens permitted in a group.
+   The common experiment allows 1,024 fast tokens and up to 2,048 slow tokens
+   *per layer*. These are controlled research budgets, not the GPU's full size.
+
+A **layer** is one stage of the model. The model has many stages; each keeps
+its own K/V. A **key** helps attention decide which earlier tokens matter.
+A **value** carries the information attention uses from a chosen token. You
+do not need the equations to follow the cache policy: think of each token as
+having a paired K and V that must stay together.
+
+**Three different things that can be easy to mix up:**
+
+| Word in a result | What it tells us | What it does not tell us |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Logical fast/slow slots | How many token positions the *policy* allows in each group | How much physical GPU or CPU memory a run uses |
+| Logical writes saved | How many slow-tier write events the policy avoids | How many writes an STT-RAM chip actually performs |
+| Measured GPU memory | Memory used by this running program | Memory a future STT-RAM device would need |
+
+## What actually happens
+
+```text
+Prompt arrives
+  → model creates all prompt K/V on GPU
+  → policy scores prompt tokens
+  → some K/V goes into the fast group, some into the slow group, rest is dropped
+  → model starts generating one token at a time
+  → attention sees fast tokens plus at most 32 selected slow tokens
+  → attention scores can make an exposed slow token eligible for promotion
+  → new token enters fast group; an old fast token may be demoted
+  → repeat
+```
+
+The policy always keeps a few tokens at the beginning (called **attention
+sinks**) and a recent window in the fast group. It ranks other prompt tokens
+using an approximate importance score. At generation time, it uses the
+model's measured attention to update importance. Each model layer makes its
+own placement decisions.
+
+A slow token is **not automatically searched every step**. The code selects
+up to 32 slow tokens to expose to attention, largely using their recorded
+recent access. An unexposed token cannot gain new attention evidence in that
+step. This helps explain why retaining a token does not guarantee that the
+model will use it at the right time.
+
+## A small example you can simulate
+
+Imagine a toy fast group with room for 3 tokens and a slow group with room for
+2. After scoring a six-token prompt, suppose the policy keeps tokens A, B, C
+fast; D, E slow; and drops F. The actual program uses sink, recent-window and
+importance rules to make this choice; these letters are only an illustration.
+
+1. The next answer step attends to A, B, C and perhaps D. It cannot attend to
+   E unless E is among the selected slow tokens. It can no longer use F.
+2. Suppose the model pays high attention to D. On the following cache update,
+   D can be **promoted** to the fast group. Because the fast group has a hard
+   limit of 3, another token must leave it.
+3. Suppose B leaves. B is **demoted** to the slow group. If the slow group is
+   full, the policy first tries to remove a duplicate backup; otherwise a
+   slow token must be dropped.
+4. When D moved up, TieredKV may have kept its old slow copy as a **shadow**.
+   If D later moves down again and that shadow still exists, the policy can
+   reuse it without writing D's K/V a second time. This is the project's
+   measured *logical write-saving* mechanism.
+
+The code's exact ordering, tie rules and per-layer tensors are documented in
+[explanation.md](explanation.md). The example above is for understanding, not
+an experimental result.
+
+## Why the results are mixed
+
+| Question | What we know today |
+| --- | --- |
+| Does shadowing save writes? | **Yes, in the logical cache counter and an opt-in CPU copy test.** On one fixed 50-step input stream it recorded 2,690 writes with shadows versus 26,432 without (89.82% fewer). In ordinary CPU memory, explicit decode K/V copied from GPU fell from 108.27 MB to 11.02 MB. This does not measure STT-RAM hardware writes or device lifetime. |
+| Is answer quality better than faithful SnapKV? | **Not proven.** Across 135 selected requests the difference was about −0.046 task-balanced points; the uncertainty interval spans both a loss and a gain. |
+| Is it faster? | **No in the current comparison.** On one 16,000-token prompt and 50 generated tokens, TieredKV took 82.76 milliseconds per generated token versus 25.77 for SnapKV. |
+| Does it reduce the highest GPU memory use? | **Not yet in the default path.** The full prompt KV is created before compression, so the measured prefill peak stayed about 18.59 GB in the 16K probe. |
+| Does a real CPU tier work? | **Functionally, in a small opt-in test.** The same predictions and cache decisions were observed; final GPU allocation fell about 219 MB, but decoding slowed. CPU memory is not STT-RAM. |
+
+“Task-balanced” means each tested task gets equal weight when scores are
+averaged. It does not mean every individual example improved. The source
+artifacts and limitations are linked from [PROJECT_STATUS.md](PROJECT_STATUS.md).
+
+The speed result is **time per new token**. Lower is better: 82.76
+milliseconds is slower than 25.77 milliseconds. The 89.82% write reduction
+is a different measurement; saving writes did not make this program faster.
+
+The quality number is a benchmark score, **not** the percentage of answers
+that were correct. A difference near zero means the methods scored similarly
+on this sample. The reported 95% interval includes both a TieredKV loss and
+a gain, so the test has not settled which method is better. “3.21× slower”
+refers to one specified speed test; it is not a universal speed ratio.
+
+## What we still need before a strong paper claim
+
+The priority is to make an actual second memory tier useful *after* counting
+its transfer and policy costs. Then we need a fair comparison at comparable
+real memory use and comparable attention work; quality checks on new documents
+and another model; whole-request latency and throughput; and evidence about
+physical traffic, energy and endurance. The complete checklist is in
+[PROJECT_STATUS.md](PROJECT_STATUS.md). A date on a paper plan is not proof
+that these results exist.
+
+Another published method, [FlexiCache](https://proceedings.mlsys.org/paper_files/paper/2026/file/94bcb01789fccf15afe2764d8fe0f40e-Paper-Conference.pdf),
+also keeps a CPU copy and avoids repeatedly sending the same saved page to
+CPU memory. Our write-saving number is therefore an interesting result to
+test further, not enough by itself to claim a new invention.
+
+**A recent concrete failure:** One long question asked for five names.
+TieredKV answered only three because the sentence listing all five had been
+dropped from its saved cache in every model layer. Forcing that known sentence
+to stay fixed this one answer, but a real system cannot know the answer in
+advance. A method that searched for a useful sentence using only the question
+looked promising on 24 questions, then failed to confirm the gain on 24 new
+questions. It has **not** replaced the current method. This is why we test
+new ideas on different documents before calling them improvements.
+
+## Words and abbreviations
+
+| Term | Plain meaning here |
+| --- | --- |
+| LLM | Large language model: the program generating text. |
+| KV / KV cache | Saved **k**eys and **v**alues for earlier tokens, used by attention. |
+| GPU | Graphics processing unit: runs most model calculations. |
+| VRAM | Memory attached to the GPU; fast, but limited. |
+| CPU DRAM | Ordinary system memory attached to the processor, used only in an opt-in cold-tier experiment. |
+| STT-RAM | A real memory technology proposed as TieredKV's future slow tier. No such device is used in current model runs. |
+| Hot / cold | A token currently kept in the fast / slow cache group. |
+| Promotion / demotion | Moving a retained token from slow to fast / fast to slow. In the default runs these are moves between GPU tensors. |
+| Shadow | An extra slow copy retained when a token is promoted, so a later demotion may reuse it. |
+| Eviction / drop | Removing a token because capacity is full. Once dropped, this implementation cannot fetch it back. |
+| bf16 | A two-byte number format used to store model K/V in the main comparison. |
+| ITL / TPOT | Time per generated token during decoding; lower is faster. |
+| TTFT | Time to the first generated token, including prompt processing. |
+| p95 | A value at or below which 95% of measured cases fall; shows slow-tail behavior. |
+| SOTA | “State of the art,” meaning the strongest published results; TieredKV has not established such a claim. |
+| Baseline | Another method measured on the same task so we have something to compare against. |
+| SnapKV | A published KV-cache method used as one of our comparison methods. |
+| Logical write | A cache-policy counter saying that a K/V copy would be written to the slow group; it does not prove that physical memory hardware performed a write. |
+| Paired comparison | The two methods answer the same test requests, so their scores can be compared request by request. |
+| 95% interval | A range showing uncertainty in an estimated difference from the tested sample; if it crosses zero, this result alone does not establish a winner. |
+| Attended width | How many stored token positions attention can use in a step. More positions can help quality but can add work. |
+| Resident bytes | The actual memory occupied by stored K/V. Comparing equal token counts can still use unequal bytes. |
+
+The previous, outdated version of these notes is preserved at
+`results/archive/SIMPLE_NOTES_20260921.txt` for history. Do not use its old
+scores or speed numbers as current evidence.
